WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:01.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
As companies start

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:04.004
to collect large amounts of data
from an ever-increasing number

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:05.700
of locations, building
a data pipeline

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.230
can be quite the challenge.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.660
Join us as we talk to Two Sigma
about how they architected

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.790
a data pipeline to
fit their many demands

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.080
on this episode of Stack Chat.

00:00:16.384 --> 00:00:17.300
Thanks for joining us.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.008
Tell us a little bit
more about Two Sigma

00:00:19.008 --> 00:00:21.280
and why your data pipeline
was so complex to build.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:21.550
TONY WALKER: Sure.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.008
So at Two Sigma, we make use
of data science and technology

00:00:24.008 --> 00:00:25.210
to uncover value.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.400
Essentially, we can buy
massive amounts of data

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.740
along with computing power
and financial expertise

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.990
to develop sophisticated
training models.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.920
We need to ingest and process
data at an ever-increasing rate

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.730
whilst protecting it
from infiltration,

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.180
either accidental or malicious.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:38.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So can you show us

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.690
how you were able to keep
up with the demands of so

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:41.510
much data?

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.260
TONY WALKER: Sure.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.720
Let's take a look at a diagram.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.150
Our first attempt at this was
to have a GCP project which

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:47.701
was connected to the internet.

00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:49.450
This allowed us to
arrange for vendor data

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.867
to be ingested from various
sources, such as S3 or FTP.

00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:54.700
This enabled us to have
an environment where

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.340
our researchers were able to
explore datasets and discover

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:58.594
new features.

00:00:58.594 --> 00:01:00.760
From there, our engineers
could build data pipelines

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.850
using native services like
BigQuery, Google Cloud Storage,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.560
and Dataproc or Dataflow.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.510
This enabled us to
rapidly derive insights

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.760
from the data in a
more scalable manner

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.260
when compared to our
on-premise environment.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:12.850
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
You mentioned earlier

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.350
that you needed to
protect the data.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.016
Can you tell us more
about how you dealt

00:01:16.016 --> 00:01:17.290
with that in this environment?

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.500
TONY WALKER: As you can
imagine, the security

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.250
of our intellectual property,
both internal and external,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.766
is of paramount importance.

00:01:22.766 --> 00:01:25.390
As a result, any operations that
deal with the movement of data

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:26.860
in or out of the
company's network

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.820
must be as secure as possible.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.820
So the most significant
concern with this design

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.460
was the fact that the project
was connected to the internet.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.480
Although this made it
easy to ingest data,

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.270
it also forced us to
isolate our engineers

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:38.581
within the environment.

00:01:38.581 --> 00:01:41.080
We essentially allowed them to
use native services, but only

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.496
from instances
within the project,

00:01:42.496 --> 00:01:44.967
essentially creating
a DMZ-like setup.

00:01:44.967 --> 00:01:47.050
This was a pain because
it required us to maintain

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.940
two separate IM identities--

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.460
one with full privileges, and
one with heavy restrictions

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.877
that could be used
safely on site.

00:01:52.877 --> 00:01:55.335
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You mentioned
handling the import of data

00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:56.564
from lots of locations.

00:01:56.564 --> 00:01:57.730
How did you accomplish this?

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.355
And did you see any
impact from dealing

00:01:59.355 --> 00:02:00.500
with the size of this data?

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.350
TONY WALKER: So dealing with
multiple sources was relatively

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.260
easy.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:04.910
In some cases, we
had automated jobs,

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.595
which would collect data
from sources such as FTP.

00:02:07.595 --> 00:02:09.970
In many other cases, we were
able to leverage the storage

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.200
transfer service for
interacting with vendors

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.885
which share the data via S3.

00:02:13.885 --> 00:02:15.760
This was great, as it
removed the need for us

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.100
to build anything
bespoke for the job.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.308
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Going back
to security, how did you

00:02:20.308 --> 00:02:22.683
ensure that only the right
people could access your data?

00:02:22.683 --> 00:02:24.141
TONY WALKER: The
key change we made

00:02:24.141 --> 00:02:26.420
is that the product is now
part of a secure perimeter.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.200
This is a concept from
the secure VPC product.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.690
Essentially, it allows us
to have fine-grained control

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.010
over who has access to data
stored within BigQuery and GCS.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.510
The other key difference is
that we use private interconnect

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.400
between Two Sigma and Google.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.320
With that in place, we
could enable engineers

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.200
and researchers to safely access
the data using native tools

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.670
directly from within Two
Sigma, without the risk

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.342
it could be shared outside.

00:02:48.342 --> 00:02:50.050
The other bonus with
private interconnect

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.996
was that we had dedicated
bandwidth for data transfers.

00:02:52.996 --> 00:02:55.120
Ultimately, the new
architecture was a game changer

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.161
for our data engineering team.

00:02:57.161 --> 00:02:58.660
They could now
operate independently

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.980
without impacting anyone
else within the company.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Now, this has
been running well for a while.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.570
And you've learned
a lot along the way.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.170
If you could go back
and do it again,

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.850
what would you do differently?

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.340
TONY WALKER: Well, one thing
we've rather glossed over

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.320
so far is the way in
which we ingest data

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:12.730
in the new environment.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.589
The secure VPC product
makes it very easy

00:03:14.589 --> 00:03:16.630
to lock down where our
data can be accessed from,

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:18.820
but it also introduces a
new level of complexity

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.504
when dealing with getting
data from external sources.

00:03:21.504 --> 00:03:23.170
This also required
us to think carefully

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.140
about how secure API traffic
is routed between Two

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.120
Sigma and these projects.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.460
In order to do this, we
implemented a secure VPC

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.105
concept called bridging.

00:03:32.105 --> 00:03:33.730
This allowed us to
use a single product

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.920
as the gateway for all
other secure traffic, which

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.720
has eased management,
but presents us

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.790
with a new bottleneck,
which we have concerns

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.005
with in the long-run in
terms of scalability.

00:03:42.005 --> 00:03:43.630
This is something
we'll look to address

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:45.310
as we on-board more teams.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.440
So by architecting
carefully, we were

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.200
able to handle large volumes
of unpredictable external data,

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.900
combine those with our internal
data sets, and stay secure.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:53.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thanks
so much for giving us

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.310
some insight into how you built
your data pipeline to handle

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.430
all of these challenges.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:58.472
TONY WALKER: My pleasure.

00:03:58.472 --> 00:04:01.096
MARK MIRCHANDANI: If you'd like
to get some hands-on experience

00:04:01.096 --> 00:04:03.110
and try setting up a VPC
perimeter for yourself,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.317
check out the quick
guide in the description.

00:04:06.317 --> 00:04:07.150
Thanks for watching.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.100
And make sure to subscribe
to the Google Cloud Platform

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.487
channel for more great content.

00:04:10.487 --> 00:04:12.070
We'll see you next
time on Stack Chat.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.220
